-- | Two-phase attempts: a record that survives a revert.
--
-- THE OBJECTION THIS EXISTS FOR. Arpit Karnatak of Vacuumlabs, on
-- forum.canton.network/t/9084, 18 October 2026:
--
--   "We can commit when a check fails, but then there can be a case when
--    checks pass but something else fails/reverts later on in the
--    transaction, and that will not be committed and cause inconsistencies
--    for what goes onchain, if we use chain as the source of truth."
--
-- He is right, and Journal.daml's `Allow` has exactly that hole. The count is
-- taken in the same transaction as the work, so a revert takes the count with
-- it. The ledger then under-reports attempts and an auditor dividing by it is
-- dividing by a number that quietly lost its losers.
--
-- WHY IT CANNOT BE FIXED IN ONE TRANSACTION. Daml commits whole or not at
-- all. There is no way for a record written inside a transaction to survive
-- that transaction aborting; that is atomicity, not a missing feature. So any
-- fix MUST span more than one transaction. Once you accept that, the shape is
-- forced:
--
--   TX1  open the attempt. It contains nothing else, so nothing can revert it.
--   TX2  do the work, consuming the attempt. If this reverts, TX1 still stands.
--
-- An attempt left OPEN is therefore the evidence that something was tried and
-- did not complete. The absence of a resolution is the record. That is the
-- whole trick, and it is why this works where a same-transaction write cannot.
--
-- PRIOR ART, named up front so nobody has to raise it.
--
-- The mechanism is WRITE-AHEAD LOGGING. The defining WAL property is that the
-- intention is made durable BEFORE the mutation, so a crash between the two
-- leaves a readable record of what was going to happen. That is transaction 1
-- exactly. Canonical reference: Mohan, Haderle, Lindsay, Pirahesh and
-- Schwarz, "ARIES", ACM TODS 17(1):94-162, 1992. The only difference here is
-- the consumer of the durability: WAL makes the intention durable so the
-- SYSTEM can recover; this makes it durable so a THIRD PARTY can audit. Same
-- mechanism, opposite reader.
--
-- The two-transaction shape in Daml is the PROPOSE-ACCEPT pattern, which is
-- documented and standard. It exists there to solve an authority problem: no
-- party can unilaterally create a contract binding another. This reuses the
-- shape for a durability-of-evidence problem instead. Credit where it is due;
-- the shape is not ours.
--
-- Two-phase commit's prepare phase is a weaker analogue, because a prepare
-- record is a participant-local artefact meant to be forgotten after commit,
-- not evidence meant to be read later. The SAGA pattern (Garcia-Molina and
-- Salem, SIGMOD 1987) is NOT the analogue and should not be cited as one: it
-- is about compensating actions restoring consistency, not about retaining
-- evidence.
--
-- Hyperledger Fabric already retains failed transactions on its ledger with a
-- validation code. Anyone who knows Fabric will say so, and they are right.
--
-- So what, if anything, is actually ours: using the UNRESOLVED record as the
-- artefact rather than as a recovery aid, and pairing it with a commitment so
-- the resolution cannot lie about what the attempt was. That is a modest
-- claim and it is the one that survives.
--
-- WHY THE AUDITOR STILL CANNOT SEE WHAT YOU DID. An attempt discloses its
-- position, its time and a `commitment`, and nothing else. The commitment is
-- sha256 over the action and detail, so it reveals nothing and fixes
-- everything: at resolution time the refusal must carry an action and detail
-- that hash to it, or the choice fails.
--
-- So the operator cannot open an attempt to move 90,000 and then resolve it
-- as a refused request for 10. The ledger will not let them. That is the same
-- non-equivocation property the receipt chain gets from SPEC 6c, enforced
-- here by the ledger instead of by a verifier.
--
-- WHAT AN AUDITOR RECONCILES.
--
--     attempts            50   from the journal
--     refusal records     12   disclosed to them
--     still open           3   tried, never resolved: reverted or in flight
--     ------------------------
--     settled             35   allowed, and they learn nothing else about them
--
-- Every number on the left comes off the ledger. None of them is the
-- operator's word.
--
-- WHEN NOT TO USE THIS. If the work cannot revert after the check, one
-- transaction is simpler and cheaper, and `Journal.Allow` is correct. Use
-- this when the work can fail for reasons the check does not cover, which in
-- payments and settlement is most of the time.
--
-- WHAT IT STILL DOES NOT DO. An open attempt cannot distinguish "the work
-- reverted" from "the work is still in flight" without a timeout the
-- application chooses. And nothing here forces an application to open an
-- attempt before acting; that is a design contract, same as the journal.
module Attempt where

import DA.Crypto.Text (sha256, toHex)
import DA.Text qualified as T

import Refusal

-- | What an attempt commits to, without disclosing it.
--
-- A COMMITMENT SCHEME NEEDS TWO PROPERTIES AND THE FIRST VERSION HAD ONE.
--
-- Binding: the committer cannot later open it to a different value. Second
-- preimage resistance gives that, and the first version had it.
--
-- Hiding: a reader cannot learn the value from the commitment. The first
-- version did NOT have it, and the claim that an open attempt revealed
-- nothing was false. `sha256("8:withdrawamount=900000")` is brute-forced in
-- seconds by anyone who guesses the format, and payment actions are exactly
-- the low-entropy case where that works: a handful of verbs and round
-- amounts. The whole input space is enumerable.
--
-- So the commitment takes a `salt`, which the opener generates at random and
-- keeps OFF the ledger, revealing it only at resolution. Hiding is restored
-- and binding is untouched.
--
-- The salt must be unpredictable and fresh per attempt. Reuse it and two
-- attempts over the same action collide visibly, which hands a reader the
-- equality they were not supposed to learn.
--
-- An operator who loses a salt cannot resolve that attempt. It stays open,
-- which the auditor reads as unresolved, which is honest: an attempt nobody
-- can account for is exactly what an unresolved attempt means.
--
-- Length-prefixed so the split is unambiguous: without it, action "ab" with
-- detail "c" and action "a" with detail "bc" would commit to the same digest
-- and an operator could move the boundary after the fact. The salt is
-- length-prefixed for the same reason.
--
-- `toHex` first because Daml's `sha256` takes a hex string, not arbitrary
-- text. So the digest is sha256 over the UTF-8 bytes of the prefixed string,
-- which is what any other language computes from those bytes.
attemptCommitment : Text -> Text -> Text -> Text
attemptCommitment salt action detail =
  sha256 (toHex (
    show (T.length salt) <> ":" <> salt <>
    show (T.length action) <> ":" <> action <> detail))

-- | How an attempt ended. Deliberately says nothing about WHAT it was.
data Outcome
  = Settled
    -- ^ The work completed. The auditor learns no more than this.
  | Refused
    -- ^ A rule stopped it. The RefusalRecord carries the detail.
  | Abandoned
    -- ^ Closed on purpose without acting, with a reason on the record.
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- | Proof that an attempt ended, and how.
--
-- WHY THIS EXISTS. Daml gives every template an implicit `Archive` choice
-- controlled by its signatories. `owner` and `actor` are both signatories,
-- and in the ordinary case one firm controls both parties. So without this,
-- the subject of the evidence can simply archive an open attempt and an
-- auditor querying active contracts sees a clean ledger.
--
-- Every legitimate ending now mints one of these. The auditor's arithmetic
-- then closes:
--
--     attempts      50   monotonic counter, cannot be rewound
--     resolutions   47   these markers
--     still open     2
--     ----------------
--     unaccounted    1   an attempt archived with no resolution
--
-- That last line is the finding. Destroying the evidence leaves a hole with
-- a number on it.
--
-- It leaks nothing new. An auditor who can count already knew that attempts
-- minus refusals minus open equals settled; this only makes it checkable
-- per position instead of in aggregate.
template Resolution
  with
    owner : Party
    actor : Party
    auditor : Optional Party
    n : Int
      -- ^ The position of the attempt this closes.
    outcome : Outcome
      -- ^ Which way it went. Not what it was.
    resolvedAt : Time
  where
    signatory owner, actor
    observer optional [] (\a -> [a]) auditor
    ensure n >= 1

-- | One attempt, opened before the work and resolved after it.
--
-- Left open, this IS the evidence that something was tried and did not
-- complete. Resolved, it is archived and the resolution says which way it
-- went.
template Attempt
  with
    owner : Party
      -- ^ Accountable for the system. Signatory.
    actor : Party
      -- ^ Who is trying. Also signatory, so neither can later say the other
      -- opened it alone.
    auditor : Optional Party
      -- ^ Named in advance, as everywhere else here. Sees that an attempt
      -- happened and when. Does not see what it was.
    n : Int
      -- ^ Position, taken from the journal in the same transaction that
      -- opened this. The number the auditor divides by.
    commitment : Text
      -- ^ `attemptCommitment action detail`. Binds this attempt to one
      -- specific action without revealing it, so a resolution cannot
      -- describe a different one.
    openedAt : Time
      -- ^ Ledger effective time, not a clock the submitter writes.
  where
    signatory owner, actor

    observer optional [] (\a -> [a]) auditor

    ensure n >= 1 && commitment /= ""

    -- | The rule said no. Archives the attempt and writes the refusal, which
    -- must match what was committed to.
    --
    -- This is the only choice that reveals the action, and it reveals it only
    -- on the branch where the auditor is entitled to see it.
    choice RefuseAttempt : (ContractId RefusalRecord, ContractId Resolution)
      with
        salt : Text
        action : Text
        detail : Text
        rule : Text
      controller owner, actor
      do
        assertMsg
          "the refusal does not match what the attempt committed to"
          (attemptCommitment salt action detail == commitment)
        now <- getTime
        record <- create RefusalRecord with
          owner; actor; action; detail; rule
          refusedAt = now
          auditor
        marker <- create Resolution with
          owner; actor; auditor; n
          outcome = Refused
          resolvedAt = now
        pure (record, marker)

    -- | The work succeeded. Archives the attempt and writes nothing about
    -- what it was.
    --
    -- The auditor's whole view of an allowed attempt is that it opened and
    -- then stopped being open. No AcceptanceRecord, deliberately. Adding one
    -- would hand over the actions that were allowed, which is the thing this
    -- design exists to avoid.
    --
    -- The caller does its real work in this same transaction. If that work
    -- reverts, this choice reverts with it and the attempt stays open, which
    -- is the entire point.
    choice SettleAttempt : ContractId Resolution
      with
        salt : Text
        action : Text
        detail : Text
      controller owner, actor
      do
        assertMsg
          "the settlement does not match what the attempt committed to"
          (attemptCommitment salt action detail == commitment)
        now <- getTime
        create Resolution with
          owner; actor; auditor; n
          outcome = Settled
          resolvedAt = now

    -- | Closed on purpose rather than left dangling. Says so on the record.
    --
    -- An operator who abandons attempts honestly looks better than one whose
    -- ledger is full of open attempts they will not explain, and the auditor
    -- can tell the two apart.
    choice AbandonAttempt : ContractId Resolution
      with
        reason : Text
      controller owner, actor
      do
        assertMsg "an abandonment needs a reason" (reason /= "")
        now <- getTime
        create Resolution with
          owner; actor; auditor; n
          outcome = Abandoned
          resolvedAt = now

    -- | What the auditor can read without asking the operator: that this
    -- attempt exists, its position, and when it opened. Non-consuming.
    nonconsuming choice Opened : (Int, Time)
      controller optional owner identity auditor
      do pure (n, openedAt)
